Q: What is Panache? (Spring Data JPA alternative sugar)

Panache is Quarkus’s opinionated layer on Hibernate ORM that reduces boilerplate.
Two styles:

────────────────────────────────────────
1) Active Record (entity has helpers)

@Entity
public class Book extends PanacheEntity {
    public String title;
    public String author;
}

// usage
Book.listAll();
Book.findById(1L);
Book.find("author", "Ali").list();
book.persist();
book.delete();

Feels different from Spring (where entity is usually “dumb” and repo has methods).

────────────────────────────────────────
2) Repository style (closer to Spring Data)

@ApplicationScoped
public class BookRepository implements PanacheRepository<Book> {
    public List<Book> findByAuthor(String author) {
        return list("author", author);
    }
}

@Inject
BookRepository bookRepository;

Closer to `JpaRepository` mental model.

────────────────────────────────────────
Transactions

Use:
jakarta.transaction.Transactional

@Transactional
public void create(Book b) {
    b.persist();
}

Same idea as Spring `@Transactional`, different package/manager (JTA/Narayana under Quarkus).

────────────────────────────────────────
Panache vs Spring Data JPA

| Spring Data                      | Panache                          |
| -------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `extends JpaRepository`          | `PanacheRepository` or entity AR |
| Derived query methods            | `find("field", value)` / queries |
| Entities usually anemic          | Active Record optional           |

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview one-liner

“Panache simplifies Hibernate in Quarkus with Active Record or repository helpers — less boilerplate than classic JPA, similar role to Spring Data repositories.”
